WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.250
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good morning, ladies and
gentlemen, and welcome to this meeting of

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:15.750
the Board of NHS England. As we all know,
we meet in public, and I would like this morning

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:21.119
to welcome to our room here in Skipton House
in London members of the public who have joined

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:27.140
us, and also all of those who are watching
us through the live stream. It is a meeting

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.640
in public, not a public meeting, but we will
have an opportunity this morning to discuss

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.030
some business that lies at the heart of the
work of the National Health Service in England.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.830
I would like in particular to welcome two
who will be joining our panel during the course

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:48.940
of our deliberations this morning. The first
is Sir&nbsp;Muir Gray and the second is Professor&nbsp;Tim

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.360
Briggs, who will help us to understand the
work that has been undertaken through the

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.160
RightCare initiative, and also through the
Getting It Right First Time initiative. It

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.800
is a unique opportunity for the Board to meld
together in our consciousness these two streams

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:10.970
of work, and to understand what their implications
are for the NHS more generally. Everybody

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.050
is welcome; I declare the meeting open.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.650
We have apologies this morning from Lord&nbsp;Victor
Adebowale, who is unable to be with us, but

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.700
he was with us yesterday, and I would just
stress that yesterday we had, as we usually

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.010
do, a development session, in which we consider
a number of items of forthcoming business.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.680
The Board meets informally and deals with
strategic issues which are in the course of

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.960
formulation for items that will come ultimately
formally to the Board for approval, and the

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.380
Chief Executive will in a moment talk about
one or two of those items that are coming

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.380
through the system. Yesterday, we also had
a meeting of the Commissioning Committee,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:04.410
so this meeting fits within an overall context
of a scheduled series of meetings, and we

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:09.229
will also have a private Board meeting in
which we take business which, for reasons

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:17.909
of commercial, patient or other confidentiality,
cannot at the moment be taken in public.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:24.629
Let me then kick off with a request for declarations
of interest. We have tabled in front of every

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:30.590
member of the Board our standing list of interests,
which we will take as being formally declared,

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.370
but I think there may be one or two colleagues
who wish to make a declaration this morning

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.840
in respect of business that is coming up on
the agenda, so let me invite that.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.829
Joanne Shaw: I would like to declare an interest
in paper number five, on conflicts of interest

00:02:44.829 --> 00:02:50.919
in the NHS, which is due to the fact that,
as you know, I am Deputy Chairman of the charity

00:02:50.919 --> 00:02:57.970
Nuffield Health, which provides independent
healthcare, including that provided by NHS

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:08.780
consultants working in private practice.
John Burn: I remind the Board that I am the

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.400
Chairman of QuantuMDx group, which is involved
in warfarin genotyping development, as one

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.700
of its point of care tests, and I was planning
to ask about that in the RightCare discussion.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.239
Malcolm Grant: I take the view that neither
of those declarations disqualify the members

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:30.460
of the Board from participating in the discussion,
so are we comfortable with that? Okay. With

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.599
appropriate disciplinary action, of course,
should they step beyond the mark.

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:40.159
May we then move on to item two on the agenda,
which is the minutes of the previous meeting.

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:45.349
May I invite the Board to approve the minutes?
I have no matters arising from them; are there

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:53.019
any matters arising? Right, thank you very
much.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:59.339
Just a few issues for me to report informally.
The first is that I have accepted an interim

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:05.900
appointment to a board which will be the transition
board in light of the government’s proposal

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:13.729
to set up a new property company, which will
be merging the existing services under property

00:04:13.729 --> 00:04:20.970
services and CHP, but also superimposing across
it a strategic estates capability. I have

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:27.870
agreed to sit on the transitional newco board
as we drive this through to a new model. Final

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.289
corporate structure to be determined and composition
of the board to be determined, but this is

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:39.930
an interim move in order for us to be able
to reflect NHSE’s interests in the new model.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:47.670
Secondly, to report that I have accepted an
appointment to chair a commission on London’s

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:52.199
roads and streets, which is being led from
the Centre for London, and in which I have

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:57.100
a particular interest, not only in terms of
urban planning and layout, but also around

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:03.900
health, air pollution, exercise. This is being
done by an independent centre, but also in

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:10.039
close collaboration with the Deputy Mayor
for Transport for London and other stakeholders

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:17.729
across London. That is a short-term commission;
it should take no more than nine months to

00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:21.050
come up with proposals.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:25.930
In addition, over the last two months or so
since our last meeting, I have been involved

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:32.460
in extensive meetings with colleagues at NHS
Improvement and elsewhere across the arm’s-length

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:39.919
bodies, and many of these jointly with the
Chief Executive. I have also been working

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:46.840
closely as a member of the Board of Genomics
England Ltd, as we develop proposals to take

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:53.640
the next stage of development of whole genome
sequencing out of what is at the moment predominantly

00:05:53.640 --> 00:06:01.499
a research and test of concept context, into
what we will be inviting the Board to consider

00:06:01.499 --> 00:06:08.379
at its next meeting: a proposal to introduce
whole genome sequencing as a more routine

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:14.470
clinical test within the NHS, something which
will take a lot of complexity, a lot of time

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:18.500
and a lot of development to bring through,
but which will allow us to reap the benefits

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.820
of the government’s investment in the 100,000
Genomes project.

